drivers/virtio/mmio: skip cmdline devices that do not attach

The command-line probe gives up on the whole bus as soon as one device
fails to attach, so a single device Unikraft has no driver for hides
every device declared after it:

    ERR:  [libvirtio_mmio] Failed to find the driver for the virtio
          device (id:5)
    ERR:  [libvirtio_mmio_cmdl] Could not add device (-14)

That is how a VMM lays out its bus, not an error: libkrun always creates
a virtio-balloon (id 5) and a virtio-entropy (id 4) ahead of the devices
the caller asked for, and Unikraft has drivers for neither — so a
virtio-fs volume behind them is never registered, and mounting it fails
with -ENOENT long before the application runs.

The FDT probe already treats this as expected (pf_probe_fdt frees the
device and continues to the next node); this makes the command-line
probe, the only discovery path on x86_64, behave the same. A device
whose MMIO region holds no device at all reports the same failure, so
declaring more slots than the VMM populated stays harmless too.

Found against unikraft 0.21.0 (Ijiraq).
--- a/drivers/virtio/mmio/virtio_mmio_cmdl.c
+++ b/drivers/virtio/mmio/virtio_mmio_cmdl.c
@@ -100,9 +100,9 @@

 		rc = virtio_mmio_add_dev(dev);
 		if (unlikely(rc)) {
-			uk_pr_err("Could not add device (%d)\n", rc);
+			uk_pr_info("Skipping device (%d)\n", rc);
 			free(dev);
-			return rc;
+			continue;
 		}
 	}

